1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,875
You already know how to pull columns out of a table.

2
00:00:02,875 --> 00:00:07,277
The next skill is keeping only the rows you mean, and then counting them.

3
00:00:07,277 --> 00:00:12,819
Picture an orders table: who bought, from which city, and how much they spent.

4
00:00:12,819 --> 00:00:15,125
Four rows are enough to see the pattern.

5
00:00:15,125 --> 00:00:17,375
Maya ordered twice from Austin.

6
00:00:17,375 --> 00:00:19,597
Jordan ordered once from Austin.

7
00:00:19,597 --> 00:00:22,083
Sofia ordered once from Denver.

8
00:00:22,083 --> 00:00:25,458
SELECT without a filter returns every order.

9
00:00:25,458 --> 00:00:28,277
That is fine when the table fits on a screen.

10
00:00:28,277 --> 00:00:32,208
It falls apart when the table grows to thousands of rows.

11
00:00:32,208 --> 00:00:35,291
WHERE keeps a row only when a condition is true.

12
00:00:35,291 --> 00:00:38,000
Think of it as a gate on the way out of the table.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,680
Equals compares a column to a value.

14
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:42,944
Strings sit in single quotes.

15
00:00:42,944 --> 00:00:44,236
Numbers do not.

16
00:00:44,236 --> 00:00:47,208
city equals Austin is a string test.

17
00:00:47,208 --> 00:00:50,180
total greater than twenty is a number test.

18
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,166
You can ask for both at once.

19
00:00:52,166 --> 00:00:55,111
AND requires every condition to be true.

20
00:00:55,111 --> 00:00:57,944
OR lets a row through if any condition is true.

21
00:00:57,944 --> 00:01:01,194
Parentheses keep the logic honest when you mix them.

22
00:01:01,194 --> 00:01:05,138
Without parentheses, AND usually binds tighter than OR.

23
00:01:05,138 --> 00:01:08,527
Write the grouping you mean, then read it back out loud.

24
00:01:08,527 --> 00:01:11,472
NULL is not a value you can compare with equals.

25
00:01:11,472 --> 00:01:14,250
Unknown compared to Austin is not true.

26
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:18,208
Use IS NULL when the cell is empty on purpose.

27
00:01:18,208 --> 00:01:21,152
Use IS NOT NULL when you need a real city.

28
00:01:21,152 --> 00:01:24,402
LIKE matches a pattern instead of an exact string.

29
00:01:24,402 --> 00:01:27,166
Percent means any run of characters.

30
00:01:27,166 --> 00:01:30,319
Underscore means exactly one character.

31
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:32,444
Patterns still live in quotes.

32
00:01:32,444 --> 00:01:35,277
Once the rows are honest, you summarize.

33
00:01:35,277 --> 00:01:38,611
COUNT star counts rows that survived the filter.

34
00:01:38,611 --> 00:01:40,361
It does not count columns.

35
00:01:40,361 --> 00:01:42,347
It counts surviving rows.

36
00:01:42,347 --> 00:01:45,652
SUM adds a numeric column across those rows.

37
00:01:45,652 --> 00:01:47,486
AVG takes the mean.

38
00:01:47,486 --> 00:01:49,791
MIN and MAX find the edges.

39
00:01:49,791 --> 00:01:55,000
Those functions collapse many rows into one number — unless you group.

40
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,555
GROUP BY collapses rows that share a value into one result row.

41
00:01:59,555 --> 00:02:02,916
Group by city, and Austin becomes one row.

42
00:02:02,916 --> 00:02:04,583
Denver becomes another.

43
00:02:04,583 --> 00:02:08,111
Then COUNT tells you how many orders that city produced.

44
00:02:08,111 --> 00:02:10,972
SUM tells you how much money that city produced.

45
00:02:10,972 --> 00:02:15,402
The select list may only keep the grouped column and the summaries.

46
00:02:15,402 --> 00:02:19,097
You cannot keep customer name unless you also group by it.

47
00:02:19,097 --> 00:02:22,694
That rule is the engine protecting you from a fake fact.

48
00:02:22,694 --> 00:02:25,847
HAVING filters groups after the grouping is done.

49
00:02:25,847 --> 00:02:28,402
WHERE filters rows before the group.

50
00:02:28,402 --> 00:02:30,305
That order is the whole trick.

51
00:02:30,305 --> 00:02:33,736
If you filter cities after the count, you want HAVING.

52
00:02:33,736 --> 00:02:37,305
If you filter rows before the count, you want WHERE.

53
00:02:37,305 --> 00:02:39,916
Read a summary query as a short story.

54
00:02:39,916 --> 00:02:41,694
Pick the columns you will show.

55
00:02:41,694 --> 00:02:42,902
Name the table.

56
00:02:42,902 --> 00:02:44,722
Keep the rows that belong.

57
00:02:44,722 --> 00:02:46,750
Group the ones that share a city.

58
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:48,777
Keep the groups that still matter.

59
00:02:48,777 --> 00:02:50,791
Sort if the ranking is the point.

60
00:02:50,791 --> 00:02:54,319
Try it on the four-row table before you trust a warehouse.

61
00:02:54,319 --> 00:02:57,291
Filter Austin, and two customers remain.

62
00:02:57,291 --> 00:02:59,652
One of those orders is under twenty.

63
00:02:59,652 --> 00:03:03,402
AND total greater than twenty leaves Maya's large order.

64
00:03:03,402 --> 00:03:07,888
Group the original table by city, and Austin has three orders.

65
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:09,111
Denver has one.

66
00:03:09,111 --> 00:03:12,819
HAVING COUNT star greater than two keeps only Austin.

67
00:03:12,819 --> 00:03:17,402
If a group looks empty, the filter probably ate the rows first.

68
00:03:17,402 --> 00:03:22,597
Write the WHERE, then count, then see if the story is still the one you meant.

69
00:03:22,597 --> 00:03:27,750
One idea at a time, and the table answers with a short, true list.

70
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:30,319
A filter is a decision you can defend.

71
00:03:30,319 --> 00:03:35,291
If you cannot say which rows should vanish, you are not ready to summarize.

72
00:03:35,291 --> 00:03:38,222
Totals without a filter are a census.

73
00:03:38,222 --> 00:03:40,736
Totals with a filter are an answer.

74
00:03:40,736 --> 00:03:42,875
Watch the types in the comparison.

75
00:03:42,875 --> 00:03:47,500
Quoted twenty is text, and it will not compare the way a number does.

76
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:53,402
Unquoted Austin is an identifier, and it will fail unless you have a column of that name.

77
00:03:53,402 --> 00:03:55,277
The quotes are part of the meaning.

78
00:03:55,277 --> 00:03:59,361
When a city list is long, IN keeps the WHERE readable.

79
00:03:59,361 --> 00:04:04,875
When the list comes from another query, the subquery is still a filter, not a join.

80
00:04:04,875 --> 00:04:08,583
You can filter, then join, then filter again.

81
00:04:08,583 --> 00:04:11,888
Each WHERE is still about rows, not groups.

82
00:04:11,888 --> 00:04:15,097
COUNT of a column ignores NULL in that column.

83
00:04:15,097 --> 00:04:16,500
COUNT star does not.

84
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,250
That difference shows up the first time a city is missing.

85
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:25,250
If you need distinct customers, COUNT DISTINCT customer, not COUNT star.

86
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:28,930
Three Austin orders from two people are two customers.

87
00:04:29,066 --> 00:04:30,849
Summaries want that honesty.

88
00:04:30,849 --> 00:04:35,502
ORDER BY after a group sorts the groups, not the original rows.

89
00:04:35,502 --> 00:04:37,793
That is usually what a ranking wants.

90
00:04:37,793 --> 00:04:41,224
LIMIT without ORDER BY is an arbitrary handful.

91
00:04:41,224 --> 00:04:43,307
Do not treat it as top cities.

92
00:04:43,307 --> 00:04:46,085
Put the sort in when the ranking is the point.

93
00:04:46,085 --> 00:04:48,085
Practice one change at a time.

94
00:04:48,085 --> 00:04:49,071
Add WHERE.

95
00:04:49,071 --> 00:04:50,293
See the rows.

96
00:04:50,293 --> 00:04:51,529
Add GROUP BY.

97
00:04:51,529 --> 00:04:52,835
See the counts.

98
00:04:52,835 --> 00:04:53,891
Add HAVING.

99
00:04:53,891 --> 00:04:55,543
See which groups remain.

100
00:04:55,543 --> 00:04:59,849
Then you will not blame the engine for a story you wrote in the wrong clause.

101
00:04:59,849 --> 00:05:01,238
The table was ready.

102
00:05:01,238 --> 00:05:03,002
The question had to be too.

103
00:05:03,002 --> 00:05:06,613
Start the query as a sentence you would actually ask a person.

104
00:05:06,613 --> 00:05:10,071
Which product names sell above twenty is a sentence.

105
00:05:10,071 --> 00:05:16,529
SELECT name FROM products WHERE price greater than twenty is that sentence with the small talk stripped out.

106
00:05:16,529 --> 00:05:22,682
SQL is like English with almost no small talk, and almost no freedom of word choice.

107
00:05:22,682 --> 00:05:26,460
You must use clauses, and you must put them in a legal order.

108
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:28,946
SELECT names the columns you want to see.

109
00:05:28,946 --> 00:05:31,904
FROM names the table those columns live in.

110
00:05:31,904 --> 00:05:34,529
WHERE names the test a row must pass.

111
00:05:34,529 --> 00:05:37,877
If you skip WHERE, every row is a candidate.

112
00:05:37,877 --> 00:05:43,071
If you skip SELECT's column list and use star, every column comes along.

113
00:05:43,071 --> 00:05:45,668
Star is a peek, not a contract.

114
00:05:45,668 --> 00:05:49,654
Name the columns you will use, so the result stays readable.

115
00:05:49,654 --> 00:05:57,682
Comparison operators are the tests: equals, not equals, less, greater, and the inclusive cousins.

116
00:05:57,682 --> 00:06:03,391
Not equals can be written two ways in many engines, and both mean the same test.

117
00:06:03,391 --> 00:06:05,877
Strings live in single quotes.

118
00:06:05,877 --> 00:06:07,168
Numbers do not.

119
00:06:07,168 --> 00:06:11,932
Quoted twenty is text, and a price column will not treat it as money.

120
00:06:11,932 --> 00:06:16,196
AND requires every test to be true for the row to survive.

121
00:06:16,196 --> 00:06:19,516
OR lets a row through if any listed test is true.

122
00:06:19,516 --> 00:06:20,974
NOT flips a test.

123
00:06:20,974 --> 00:06:25,988
Parentheses show the grouping you mean when AND and OR share a WHERE.

124
00:06:25,988 --> 00:06:33,446
Without them, AND usually binds tighter, and the sentence you said in your head may not be the sentence the engine ran.

125
00:06:33,446 --> 00:06:36,849
BETWEEN is a closed range: the edges count.

126
00:06:36,849 --> 00:06:41,974
IN is a list of allowed values, and it reads cleaner than a pile of ORs.

127
00:06:41,974 --> 00:06:44,766
A subquery in IN is still a filter.

128
00:06:44,766 --> 00:06:46,071
It is not a join.

129
00:06:46,071 --> 00:06:47,849
LIKE compares to a pattern.

130
00:06:47,849 --> 00:06:51,918
Percent stands for any run of characters, including none.

131
00:06:51,918 --> 00:06:55,307
Underscore stands for exactly one character.

132
00:06:55,307 --> 00:07:01,960
A pattern that starts with percent cannot use a normal prefix index well, and that is a later speed lesson.

133
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:05,585
For now, write the pattern that matches the names you mean.

134
00:07:05,585 --> 00:07:07,057
NULL is not a value.

135
00:07:07,057 --> 00:07:08,154
It is a hole.

136
00:07:08,154 --> 00:07:10,432
Equals NULL is never true.

137
00:07:10,432 --> 00:07:12,502
IS NULL is the test for the hole.

138
00:07:12,502 --> 00:07:15,252
IS NOT NULL is the test for a real cell.

139
00:07:15,252 --> 00:07:17,918
ORDER BY sorts the rows that survived.

140
00:07:17,918 --> 00:07:22,029
ASC is lowest to highest, and it is the default.

141
00:07:22,029 --> 00:07:24,141
DESC is highest to lowest.

142
00:07:24,141 --> 00:07:26,363
You can sort by more than one column.

143
00:07:26,363 --> 00:07:29,835
The second column only breaks ties in the first.

144
00:07:29,835 --> 00:07:34,682
Text sorts by the collation of the column, which is why case can surprise you.

145
00:07:34,682 --> 00:07:39,224
LIMIT without ORDER BY is an arbitrary handful, not a ranking.

146
00:07:39,224 --> 00:07:42,918
Once the rows are honest, aggregation collapses them.

147
00:07:42,918 --> 00:07:45,571
COUNT star counts surviving rows.

148
00:07:45,571 --> 00:07:48,779
COUNT of a column ignores NULL in that column.

149
00:07:48,779 --> 00:07:55,474
COUNT DISTINCT counts unique values, so three Austin orders from two people are two customers.

150
00:07:55,474 --> 00:07:57,529
SUM adds a numeric column.

151
00:07:57,529 --> 00:08:00,724
AVG takes the mean of the values it can see.

152
00:08:00,724 --> 00:08:03,029
MIN and MAX find the edges.

153
00:08:03,029 --> 00:08:06,932
ROUND is a display choice on a number you already computed.

154
00:08:06,932 --> 00:08:11,807
AS names the result column so the heading is a word a human can read.

155
00:08:11,807 --> 00:08:15,627
Those functions return one row — unless you GROUP BY.

156
00:08:15,627 --> 00:08:20,196
GROUP BY makes one result row per distinct value of the grouped column.

157
00:08:20,196 --> 00:08:25,127
Group by city, and Austin is one row even if three orders live there.

158
00:08:25,127 --> 00:08:29,363
The select list may keep the grouped columns and the summaries.

159
00:08:29,363 --> 00:08:36,085
A leftover customer name with no grouping is a fake fact: which customer owns the city's total?

160
00:08:36,085 --> 00:08:38,335
The engine is right to refuse that.

161
00:08:38,335 --> 00:08:40,210
WHERE happens before the group.

162
00:08:40,210 --> 00:08:41,793
HAVING happens after.

163
00:08:41,793 --> 00:08:43,432
Filter rows with WHERE.

164
00:08:43,432 --> 00:08:45,127
Filter groups with HAVING.

165
00:08:45,127 --> 00:08:50,474
If you want cities with more than two orders, that is HAVING COUNT star greater than two.

166
00:08:50,474 --> 00:08:56,029
If you want to ignore Denver rows before you count, that is WHERE city is not Denver.

167
00:08:56,029 --> 00:09:00,710
Read the query in engine order, not in the order you typed the words.

168
00:09:00,710 --> 00:09:02,307
FROM finds the table.

169
00:09:02,426 --> 00:09:03,848
WHERE drops rows.

170
00:09:03,848 --> 00:09:05,417
GROUP BY collapses.

171
00:09:05,417 --> 00:09:07,056
HAVING drops groups.

172
00:09:07,056 --> 00:09:09,028
SELECT shapes the columns.

173
00:09:09,028 --> 00:09:11,028
ORDER BY sorts what is left.

174
00:09:11,028 --> 00:09:12,681
LIMIT cuts the page.

175
00:09:12,681 --> 00:09:15,028
Practice on four rows you can see.

176
00:09:15,028 --> 00:09:19,056
Filter Austin over twenty and watch which order remains.

177
00:09:19,056 --> 00:09:23,639
Group the original table by city and watch Austin become three.

178
00:09:23,639 --> 00:09:26,112
Add HAVING and watch Denver leave.

179
00:09:26,112 --> 00:09:27,973
Change one clause at a time.

180
00:09:27,973 --> 00:09:31,598
If the answer is wrong, the story in a clause is wrong.

181
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:32,987
The table was ready.

182
00:09:32,987 --> 00:09:37,778
The question had to name the filter and the summary as two different moments.

183
00:09:37,778 --> 00:09:48,028
Refresher complete, aggregation complete, now the combined pattern the lecture promised: which customers spend the most, how many orders last month.

184
00:09:48,028 --> 00:09:56,876
Spending is SUM total GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY the sum DESC, after WHERE on the month if the month is a row filter.

185
00:09:56,876 --> 00:10:05,264
How many orders last month is COUNT star with WHERE on the month, no group if you want one number, GROUP BY day if you want a chart.

186
00:10:05,264 --> 00:10:07,556
Same clauses, different grain.

187
00:10:07,556 --> 00:10:10,042
Write the grain first on a sticky note.

188
00:10:10,042 --> 00:10:12,070
Then the SQL cannot wander.

189
00:10:12,070 --> 00:10:18,445
That is filtering and aggregation together, which is the whole video, not a five-minute taste.

190
00:10:18,445 --> 00:10:23,778
You have the clauses, the NULL rules, the group rules, and a table you can see.

191
00:10:23,778 --> 00:10:26,014
Ask the next question on purpose.

192
00:10:26,014 --> 00:10:27,820
Keep only the rows you mean.

193
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:29,862
Count or sum what survived.

194
00:10:29,862 --> 00:10:31,681
Do not mix those moments.

195
00:10:31,681 --> 00:10:35,320
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

196
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:38,376
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

197
00:10:38,376 --> 00:10:40,264
SELECT FROM WHERE meaning?

198
00:10:40,264 --> 00:10:42,931
Columns, table, row test.

199
00:10:42,931 --> 00:10:44,264
AND versus OR?

200
00:10:44,264 --> 00:10:46,806
All tests versus any test.

201
00:10:46,806 --> 00:10:47,917
NULL test?

202
00:10:47,917 --> 00:10:50,306
IS NULL, never equals NULL.

203
00:10:50,306 --> 00:10:51,667
GROUP BY does what?

204
00:10:51,667 --> 00:10:54,278
One result row per group value.

205
00:10:54,278 --> 00:10:55,709
HAVING versus WHERE?

206
00:10:55,709 --> 00:10:58,028
Groups after versus rows before.

207
00:10:58,028 --> 00:11:00,056
COUNT star versus COUNT column?

208
00:11:00,056 --> 00:11:02,667
Rows versus non-NULL in that column.

209
00:11:02,667 --> 00:11:03,889
COUNT DISTINCT?

210
00:11:03,889 --> 00:11:05,348
Unique values.

211
00:11:05,348 --> 00:11:07,153
LIMIT without ORDER BY?

212
00:11:07,153 --> 00:11:08,264
Not a ranking.

213
00:11:08,264 --> 00:11:09,931
Grain of a city report?

214
00:11:09,931 --> 00:11:11,431
One row per city.

215
00:11:11,431 --> 00:11:13,084
Grain of an order list?

216
00:11:13,084 --> 00:11:14,626
One row per order.

217
00:11:14,626 --> 00:11:19,348
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

